{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2728 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "8894a7033a93d7663a4f3fc43914ed5e2d29c547b11553adfb5b59d97291fdcd" :edb/db-before-t 2727 :edb/db-after-t 2728 :edb/tx-data [[13194139536040 50 #inst "2026-10-09T13:25:39.911Z" 13194139536040 true] [17592186164946 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380501 13194139536040 true] [17592186164946 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380501 13194139536040 true] [17592186164953 1104 #edb/ref 17592186164955 13194139536040 true] [17592186166892 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380514 13194139536040 true] [17592186166893 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380517 13194139536040 true] [17592186166893 1020 #edb/ref 17592186380535 13194139536040 true] [17592186167230 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536040 true] [17592186167230 1100 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-3,0] \\\\ -{x}^{2}, & x\\in (0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=2.$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186167230 1100 "$F(x) = {x^{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x ∈ [- 3, 0] \\\\ -x^{2},\\end{vmatrix}x ∈ (0, 3)$\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x = 2$.The Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x = 2$.\n\n---\n\n<a id=\"using-fourier-series-for-finding-infinite-sums\"></a>" 13194139536040 false] [17592186167230 1145 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $6$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=2.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf(2)=-{2}^{2}=-4.\n$$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186167233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380502 13194139536040 true] [17592186167233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380505 13194139536040 true] [17592186167233 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380509 13194139536040 true] [17592186167233 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536040 true] [17592186167233 1100 "Consider the function $F(x) = \\mid x \\mid$ defined on $[MATH: x ∈ [- π, π).]$ The Fourier series of the $2π$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n$f(x)∼\\frac{π}{2} - ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(4)/(π(2n - 1)^{2})\\cos ((2n - 1)x)$.\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x = 0$, we have\n$(-1)^{n + 1}$ {{field-1}}" 13194139536040 false] [17592186167233 1100 "Consider the function $F(x)=|x|$ defined on $x\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{\\pi }{2}-{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{\\pi (2n-1)^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0)={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186167233 1145 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(0)=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{4}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(2n-1)^{2}} & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380501 1034 "-4" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536040 true] [17592186380501 1111 #uuid "e4bc5c1b-f7bf-54e8-98d1-c97f6a17b100" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380503 13194139536040 true] [17592186380502 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380504 13194139536040 true] [17592186380502 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139536040 true] [17592186380502 1041 "field-2" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380502 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380503 13194139536040 true] [17592186380502 1110 #uuid "98eeca51-e29e-584b-b8f9-22358b3b8f59" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380503 1034 "continuous" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536040 true] [17592186380503 1111 #uuid "ee29e778-46aa-5082-873a-e9034ca95ca5" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380504 1034 "discontinuous" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045454 13194139536040 true] [17592186380504 1111 #uuid "e2e17038-1196-5d09-a74e-8b4578e91378" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380506 13194139536040 true] [17592186380505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380507 13194139536040 true] [17592186380505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380508 13194139536040 true] [17592186380505 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 1319413953