7592186380513 1034 "\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536040 true] [17592186380513 1111 #uuid "da75184e-3365-5f84-978a-7ed0c3ed7e1d" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380514 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380515 13194139536040 true] [17592186380514 1089 "q-343364" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380514 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536040 true] [17592186380514 1100 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2}, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 1, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=-2.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=-2.$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380514 1113 #uuid "7b2281ce-81e7-5f3f-9c3a-4eb1fa35ab9b" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380514 1145 "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is not continuous at $x=-2.$ The one-sided limits at this point are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-}(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{-}}f(x)=1 \\\\ {f}^{+}(-2) & ={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}f(x)={e}^{-2+2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\n\\frac{{f}^{-}(-2)+{f}^{+}(-2)}{2}=\\frac{1+1}{2}=1.\n$$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380516 13194139536040 true] [17592186380515 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139536040 true] [17592186380515 1041 "field-1" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380515 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380516 13194139536040 true] [17592186380515 1110 #uuid "dcc5035f-47c5-5c93-8144-85c39150c976" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380516 1034 "1" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380516 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536040 true] [17592186380516 1111 #uuid "a44c3de8-d9f4-5306-821e-984424e29656" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380518 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380523 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380526 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1017 #edb/ref 17592186380530 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1089 "q-343438" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1100 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-4,0] \\\\ 3, & x\\in (0,4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1113 #uuid "3ea02f42-e1ec-509f-a6b0-19fd2f4064ef" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380517 1145 "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-n\\pi )=(-1)^{n}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=-2,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(-2)=-1.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot (-2)}{4}) \\\\ -1 & =1+\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{8}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{2n-1} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380519 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380520 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380521 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1000 #edb/ref 17592186380522 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045451 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1041 "field-1" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1104 #edb/ref 17592186380522 13194139536040 true] [17592186380518 1110 #uuid "bd80b836-6439-51b7-944a-b2b92f850106" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380519 1034 "(-1)^{n+1}" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380519 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536040 true] [17592186380519 1111 #uuid "f9dad747-96ca-514f-8b9f-24114f85a304" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380520 1034 "1" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380520 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536040 true] [17592186380520 1111 #uuid "245e99ae-e6e7-56e0-9a04-9df79033ba42" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380521 1034 "0" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380521 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536040 true] [17592186380521 1111 #uuid "070e6852-5dc1-5ba4-8736-fcf652de2070" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380522 1034 "(-1)^{n}" 13194139536040 true] [17592186380522 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 131941395